---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Treadmill Dimensions and Size in Feet: Plan for 12 x 7 ft";
const description =
  "A full-size treadmill is about 80 x 36 in. (6.7 x 3 ft). With 6 ft behind and 2 ft each side it needs about 12 x 7 ft of floor, plus headroom and a 32 in. door.";

const FOOTPRINTS = [
  {
    type: "Under-desk walking pad",
    footprint: "50 x 22 in",
    space: "Slides under a desk or bed. No dedicated room needed.",
  },
  {
    type: "Compact folding treadmill",
    footprint: "62 x 28 in",
    space: "Folds to roughly 62 x 28 x 12 in on end against a wall.",
  },
  {
    type: "Mid-range folding treadmill",
    footprint: "72 x 34 in",
    space: "Needs 6 x 3 ft in use, about 3 x 3 ft folded and parked.",
  },
  {
    type: "Full-size home treadmill",
    footprint: "80 x 36 in",
    space: "A permanent 7 x 3 ft installation, plus clearance.",
  },
  {
    type: "Incline trainer",
    footprint: "72 x 40 in",
    space: "Shorter but far taller. Ceiling height is the binding constraint.",
  },
  {
    type: "Commercial-grade treadmill",
    footprint: "84 x 38 in",
    space: "Effectively a fixed installation. Plan delivery route first.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Buying Guides", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-of/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Treadmill Dimensions and Space", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much space do you need for a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Plan for the machine's footprint plus about 6 feet of clear floor behind it and 2 feet on each side. A typical full-size treadmill is around 80 by 36 inches, so the honest room requirement is closer to 12 feet by 7 feet. The clearance behind is a safety margin, not a comfort one — it is where you land if you come off the back.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What ceiling height do I need for a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Take your height, add the deck height of the machine, then add at least 12 inches of head clearance. Deck heights run from about 5 inches on a walking pad to 12 inches on a cushioned home machine. For a 6-foot user on a typical treadmill that means a ceiling of roughly 7 foot 9, which rules out many basements. Incline trainers add up to another 15 inches at full grade.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can a treadmill fit through a standard doorway?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most folding treadmills fit through a standard 32-inch door when folded or tipped on end, but many full-size machines are 36 to 40 inches wide across the rails and will not. Measure the narrowest point of the delivery route, including stair turns and bannisters, before ordering, and check whether the machine ships assembled or in a box.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much clearance do you need behind a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Safety guidance is consistently around 6 feet of clear space behind the rear of the belt, and 2 feet either side. This is not about comfort — it is the distance a person travels if they lose their footing and are thrown backward off the belt. A wall, a piece of furniture or a stair opening in that zone changes a stumble into an injury.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you put a treadmill upstairs or on a second floor?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually yes, structurally. A treadmill plus user is typically 400 to 600 lb spread across a large footprint, which is within the design load of a normal residential floor. The real problems upstairs are getting the machine there, floor deflection making the frame feel unstable, and noise transmitted to the room below, which is often the deciding factor.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much space does a folded treadmill take up?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A folded mid-range treadmill occupies roughly 3 feet by 3 feet of floor and stands about 5 to 6 feet tall. It is a substantial object rather than something that disappears. Folding saves real floor area — often half the in-use footprint — but plan a parking spot for it rather than assuming it will tuck into a corner.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How tall does a ceiling need to be for a 6 foot 4 person on a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Use your height plus the deck height plus 12 inches. For a 6-foot-4 (76-inch) user that is about 7 feet 11 inches on a folding treadmill with a 7-inch deck at flat, and about 8 feet 4 inches on a full-size treadmill whose deck reaches 11.7 inches at maximum incline. A standard 8-foot ceiling is not enough for running at full incline at that height; walk, keep the incline lower or choose a low-deck machine.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much space does NordicTrack say you need behind a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "NordicTrack and ProForm manuals commonly ask for at least 8 feet behind the treadmill and 2 feet on each side, more than the 2 metres (about 6.5 feet) behind and 0.5 metres each side in a Life Fitness manual summarising the ASTM F2115 standard. For an 80 by 36-inch machine, 8 feet behind means a clear area of about 14 feet 8 inches by 7 feet. Check the figure in your own model's manual.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-09-06",
      dateModified: "2026-11-28",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-09-06",
    modifiedTime: "2026-11-28",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "treadmill dimensions",
      "treadmill space requirements",
      "treadmill footprint",
      "home gym setup",
      "ceiling height",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden legacy-article">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-dims" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-dims)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated November 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Treadmill <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Dimensions</span> and Space Requirements
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          A typical full-size treadmill measures about 80 by 36 inches, roughly
          6.7 by 3 feet. With 6 feet of clearance behind it and 2 feet either
          side, plan for about 12 by 7 feet of floor, plus headroom above and a
          delivery route wide enough to get it into the room in the first place.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#footprints" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            Footprints by Type
          </a>
          <a href="#clearance" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            The Clearance Rules
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="48" height="48" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight"><a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author" class="text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a></p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Exercise Physiology &amp; Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">November 28, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="5.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-red-900">The clearance behind is not optional.</strong>
          Six feet of clear floor behind the rear roller is the standard safety
          margin, and it is the distance a person travels when thrown off the back
          of a moving belt. A wall, a radiator, a glass door or a stair opening in
          that zone is the difference between a stumble and an injury.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#footprints" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Footprints by Machine Type</a>
          <a href="#clearance" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. The Clearance Rules</a>
          <a href="#clearance-by-maker" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. Clearance by Maker and ASTM Standard</a>
          <a href="#ceiling" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Ceiling Height and Deck Height</a>
          <a href="#ceiling-by-height" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Ceiling Height by User Height, 5'6" to 6'4"</a>
          <a href="#doorways" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Getting It Into the Room</a>
          <a href="#real-sizes" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Folded and In-Use Sizes of Real Treadmills</a>
          <a href="#folded" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. What Folding Really Saves</a>
          <a href="#upstairs" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Upstairs, Basements and Garages</a>
          <a href="#measuring" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Measuring Your Room Properly</a>
          <a href="#small-rooms" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">11. If the Room Is Genuinely Small</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">12. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most treadmill returns are not about the treadmill. They are about a
          machine that arrived, would not go up the stairs, or went up the stairs
          and left no room to stand behind it. The footprint printed on the
          product page is the beginning of the space calculation, not the end of
          it, and the parts it leaves out are the parts that decide whether the
          purchase works.
        </p>

        <div id="footprints" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Footprints by Machine Type</h2>
          <p>
            These are in-use dimensions for the frame — length by width, as
            manufacturers quote them. They exclude the clearance discussed in the
            next section, which is what turns these into real room requirements.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <figure class="not-prose my-8">
        <img src="/charts/treadmill-footprint-by-type.svg" width="720" loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="w-full h-auto border border-gray-200 rounded-xl"
          alt="Bar chart of treadmill footprint by type: about 7.6 square feet for a walking pad, 12.1 for a compact folding treadmill, 17 for a mid-range folder, 20 for a full-size home treadmill or incline trainer and 22.2 for a commercial treadmill." />
        <figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-2">Machine footprint by type, in square feet, before clearance.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="not-prose my-8 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[680px] w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-gray-900 text-white">
            <tr>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Machine Type</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Typical Footprint</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">What That Means at Home</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              FOOTPRINTS.map((row, i) => (
                <tr class={i % 2 === 0 ? "bg-white" : "bg-gray-50"}>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900 align-top">{row.type}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">{row.footprint}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-500 align-top">{row.space}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Two entries deserve a warning. <a href="/incline-trainer-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Incline trainers</a> look compact because
          they are short — the deck sits at a steep angle even at zero grade — and
          then become the tallest object in the room at full incline. And
          commercial machines are quoted at a footprint that sounds manageable
          while weighing 350 lb and shipping fully assembled, which makes the
          delivery route rather than the room the real constraint.
        </p>

        <div id="clearance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Clearance Rules</h2>
          <p>
            Three numbers, and only one of them is negotiable.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Six feet behind.</strong> Measured from the rear edge of the
            belt to whatever is behind it. This is the safety zone, and the
            reasoning is unpleasant but simple: if you lose your footing at pace,
            the belt delivers you backward at the speed it was running, and you do
            not stop immediately. Manufacturers' manuals specify a figure
            of at least this much (see the next section for the exact figures) and it is the one clearance rule that should not be
            compromised, particularly in a household with children.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Two feet either side.</strong> This is about getting on and
            off safely and about being able to reach the machine to clean and
            service it. It is the number most people quietly reduce, and reducing
            it to about a foot on one side is usually survivable if the other side
            is clear. Reducing both sides means you cannot lubricate the deck or
            reach the rear bolts without moving the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Access at the front.</strong> Often forgotten entirely. You
            need to be able to open the motor hood, reach the <a href="/how-to-turn-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>power switch</a>, and
            get at the console. A machine pushed hard against a wall at the front
            makes routine maintenance a two-person furniture-moving job, which in
            practice means it does not get done.
          </p>
          <p>
            Adding it up: a full-size machine at 80 by 36 inches with proper
            clearance wants a floor area of roughly 12 feet by 7 feet. That is the
            number to measure your room against, and it is why spare bedrooms work
            and hallway alcoves generally do not.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div id="clearance-by-maker" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Clearance by Maker and ASTM Standard</h2>
          <p>
            The 6-foot figure is the round number this page uses, but it is not
            the only one in circulation, and the published rear figures are larger,
            not smaller. ICON-built machines such as NordicTrack and ProForm
            commonly ask in their manuals for at least 8 feet behind the
            treadmill and 2 feet on each side. A Life Fitness manual summarising
            ASTM F2115, the US safety standard for treadmills, gives 2 metres
            (about 79 inches, or 6.5 feet) behind and 0.5 metres (about 20
            inches) on each side. Your own manual is the figure that counts, and
            it may differ from all three.
          </p>
          <p>
            Here is what each rule means for the floor area you need, using two
            real footprints: the 80 by 36-inch full-size machine from the table
            above, and Horizon's T202 folding treadmill, which Horizon's spec table
            gives as 76 by 34 inches assembled.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-8 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[720px] w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-gray-900 text-white">
            <tr>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Clearance rule</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Behind / each side</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">80 x 36 in. treadmill needs</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Horizon T202 (76 x 34 in.) needs</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="bg-white"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900 align-top">This page's working rule</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">6 ft / 2 ft</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">152 x 84 in. (12 ft 8 in. x 7 ft)</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">148 x 82 in. (12 ft 4 in. x 6 ft 10 in.)</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900 align-top">ASTM F2115 (as summarised in a Life Fitness manual)</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">2 m (79 in.) / 0.5 m (20 in.)</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">159 x 75 in. (13 ft 3 in. x 6 ft 3 in.)</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">155 x 73 in. (12 ft 11 in. x 6 ft 1 in.)</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-white"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900 align-top">NordicTrack and ProForm manuals (common figure)</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">8 ft / 2 ft</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">176 x 84 in. (14 ft 8 in. x 7 ft)</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">172 x 82 in. (14 ft 4 in. x 6 ft 10 in.)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Two things follow. First, if you own a NordicTrack or ProForm, a room
          that is 12 feet long is not enough by the maker's own manual; plan for
          nearer 15 feet, or turn the machine to run along the longest wall or
          the diagonal. Second, the side figures differ more than the rear ones,
          and the ASTM figure of about 20 inches is the floor below which you
          should not go even on one side. None of these rules gives a figure for
          the front, so leave enough room to lift the motor hood and reach the
          power switch, as described above.
        </p>

        <div id="ceiling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Ceiling Height and Deck Height</h2>
          <p>
            The calculation nobody does until the machine is assembled. You do not
            run at your own height on a treadmill — you run at your height plus the
            deck height, and the deck is further off the floor than it looks.
          </p>
          <p>
            Deck heights vary more than you would expect. An under-desk <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>walking pad</a>
            sits about 5 inches off the floor. A typical home treadmill with real
            cushioning sits 8 to 12 inches up. Commercial machines are often 10 to
            14 inches. That is up to a foot of height you did not have standing on
            the floor.
          </p>
          <p>
            The formula is your height, plus the deck height, plus at least 12
            inches of head clearance. Twelve inches sounds generous and is not:
            running involves vertical oscillation of several inches, and a ceiling
            fan, a light fitting or a beam within that margin is a genuine hazard.
            For a 6-foot user on a 10-inch deck that means about 7 foot 10 of
            ceiling — more than many finished basements have.
          </p>
          <p>
            Incline machines change the picture again, and this is the trap. As the
            deck rises, so does your standing position, by roughly the rise of the
            front of the deck. A machine at 15 per cent grade on a 55-inch deck
            lifts the front of the deck around 8 inches higher than it does at zero; where you actually stand rises less, as the worked numbers below show. Incline trainers
            reaching 40 per cent can add over a foot. If you are buying for the
            <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">12-3-30 workout</a>
            or looking at
            <a href="/best-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline trainers</a>,
            measure your ceiling before anything else.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div id="ceiling-by-height" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Ceiling Height by User Height, 5'6" to 6'4"</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>Ceiling needed = your height + deck height + 12 inches.</strong>
            The 12 inches is Horizon Fitness's own guidance for running clearance,
            and it is the figure our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-low-ceilings/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">low-ceiling treadmill guide</a>
            uses too. The deck height is the one figure that depends on the
            machine, and it is the highest point the belt reaches, so on an incline
            treadmill use the deck height at maximum incline, not the flat one.
          </p>
          <p>
            The table runs the sum for five user heights and three real deck
            heights: about 5 inches for an under-desk walking pad, 7 1/8 inches
            for the Horizon T101's step-on height (from a retailer's spec sheet,
            close to an independent reviewer's measurement), and 11.7 inches, the
            maximum deck height Sunny Health &amp; Fitness publishes for one of its
            full-size incline treadmills at top incline. Add the thickness of any
            mat under the machine and about an inch for cushioned shoes.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-8 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[700px] w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-gray-900 text-white">
            <tr>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">User height</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Walking pad (about 5 in. deck)</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Folding treadmill, flat (7.1 in.)</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Full treadmill at max incline (11.7 in.)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="bg-white"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900 align-top">5 ft 6 in.</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">6 ft 11 in.</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">7 ft 1.1 in.</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">7 ft 5.7 in.</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900 align-top">5 ft 10 in.</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">7 ft 3 in.</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">7 ft 5.1 in.</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">7 ft 9.7 in.</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-white"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900 align-top">6 ft 0 in.</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">7 ft 5 in.</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">7 ft 7.1 in.</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">7 ft 11.7 in.</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900 align-top">6 ft 2 in.</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">7 ft 7 in.</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">7 ft 9.1 in.</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">8 ft 1.7 in.</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-white"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900 align-top">6 ft 4 in.</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">7 ft 9 in.</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">7 ft 11.1 in.</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">8 ft 3.7 in.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Read across the bottom row. A 6-foot-4 runner needs about 7 feet 11
          inches of ceiling on a typical folding treadmill at flat, and about 8
          feet 4 inches on a full-size machine at its top incline, which is more
          than a standard 8-foot room provides. A 5-foot-6 user fits under a
          7-foot-5 ceiling even at full incline. Most basements sit around 7 feet
          before ducts and beams, which is why the walking pad column is the only
          one that works for tall users there.
        </p>
        <p>
          If the maker publishes only a flat step-on height, you can estimate the
          incline figure. Most treadmills incline by lifting the front of the
          deck while the rear stays put. At 15% grade the deck tilts about 8.5
          degrees, so the front of a 60-inch deck rises about 9 inches (60 x
          0.148). You stand roughly half to two thirds of the way forward, so the
          belt under your feet rises about 4.5 to 6 inches, and that is the
          number to add to the flat step-on height. It agrees with the gap
          between the 7.1 and 11.7-inch rows above. Incline trainers that reach
          30 to 40% rise far more, and for them the maker's published maximum
          height is the only safe figure.
        </p>
        <p>
          Walkers can use a smaller margin than runners, because there is little
          bounce in a walking stride, but keep at least 6 inches between your
          head and the lowest thing above you. People speed up, stumble and reach
          up without thinking. If you are tall, also check belt length before
          ceiling: our
          <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
          gives the length to look for by height.
        </p>

        <div id="doorways" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Getting It Into the Room</h2>
          <p>
            The most common expensive surprise. A machine that fits the room
            perfectly is worth nothing if it cannot reach the room.
          </p>
          <p>
            Start with how it ships. Most home treadmills arrive in a box, part
            assembled, and the box is the object that has to fit — typically around
            75 by 35 by 12 inches and 150 to 250 lb. Boxes travel awkwardly but
            they are flat, which is a considerable advantage on a stair turn.
            Commercial machines and some premium home models arrive assembled on a
            pallet, and at that point the machine's full width is the constraint.
          </p>
          <p>
            Measure the narrowest point of the whole route, not the doorway. In
            practice the binding constraint is usually a stair turn, a bannister, a
            radiator on a landing, or the diagonal clearance at the top of a flight
            of stairs. A standard interior door is about 32 inches clear, which
            most folded or boxed machines clear and many assembled full-size
            machines, at 36 to 40 inches across the rails, do not.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two practical points. First, ask before ordering whether the delivery
            is kerbside, threshold, or room-of-choice, because kerbside delivery of
            a 200 lb box is a problem you will be solving alone on a Tuesday
            afternoon. Second, check whether the console mast detaches — on many
            machines the uprights unbolt, which reduces the width substantially and
            is often the difference between fitting and not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="real-sizes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Folded and In-Use Sizes of Real Treadmills</h2>
          <p>
            Typical footprints are useful for planning, but the machine you buy
            has its own numbers, and folded sizes vary far more than in-use ones.
            These are the dimensions makers or their listings give for six
            machines we cover elsewhere on the site, with the folded floor area
            worked out from them. Length is front to back, as the machine stands
            folded.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-8 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[760px] w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-gray-900 text-white">
            <tr>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Machine</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">In use (stated)</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Folded (stated)</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Folded floor area</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Weight</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="bg-white"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900 align-top">WalkingPad Z1 (walking pad)</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">47.6 x 15.7 in. walking area</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">31.8 x 22 x 5.4 in.</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">About 4.9 sq ft; slides under a bed</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">51 lb (WalkingPad)</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900 align-top">WalkingPad X25 (double-fold running)</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">51.9 x 19.6 in. running area</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">10.1 x 30.4 x 40.3 in.</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">About 2.1 sq ft; stands about 40 in. tall</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">104 lb (WalkingPad)</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-white"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900 align-top">Sunny SF-T7718 (low-deck folding)</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">47 x 20 in. running deck</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">63 x 29.25 x 10.5 in.</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">Stands on end against a wall</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">117.9 lb (Sunny)</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900 align-top">THERUN 16% incline (Amazon listing)</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">47.2 x 17 in. running surface</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">29.5 x 31.1 x 54.7 in.</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">About 6.4 sq ft, 55 in. tall</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">Not stated</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-white"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900 align-top">Horizon T202 (folding)</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">76 x 34 x 57 in. assembled</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">48 x 34 x 62 in.</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">About 11.3 sq ft, 62 in. tall</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">187 lb (Horizon)</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-gray-50"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900 align-top">Sole F65 (folding)</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">77 x 36 x 55 in. in use</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">48 x 36 x 66 in.</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">12 sq ft, 66 in. tall</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">Not stated on Sole's current page</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          The spread is the point. A full-size folding treadmill such as the
          Sole F65 or Horizon T202 still takes about 11 to 12 square feet of floor
          folded and stands 5 to 5 1/2 feet tall. That is more than the "about 3
          by 3 feet" rule of thumb in the folding section below: on Sole's
          figures it is 4 by 3 feet. The double-fold WalkingPad X25 and
          the Z1 pad take a fraction of that, which is the real reason to buy
          them for a small room. Sources: WalkingPad's specification tables,
          Sunny's own store, Horizon's spec table, Sole's product page (28
          October 2026) and the THERUN Amazon listing (21 November 2026).
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>For delivery, weight decides the route as much as size.</strong>
          As our guide to
          <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-weigh/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how much a treadmill weighs</a>
          sets out, walking pads weigh about 45 to 75 lb, folding home
          treadmills about 150 to 250 lb, non-folding home treadmills about 250
          to 350 lb and commercial machines 350 to 500 lb or more, and the
          shipping weight is higher again because of the carton. A 51 lb pad is a
          one-person carry up a flight of stairs; a 187 lb folder in its box is a
          two-person job, and anything heavier is worth paying for in-home
          delivery. Before ordering, find the box dimensions on the listing or ask
          the seller, measure the tightest stair turn against the longest side of
          the box, and see our guide to
          <a href="/how-to-move-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">moving a treadmill</a>
          for getting it up the stairs.
        </p>

        <div id="folded" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. What Folding Really Saves</h2>
          <p>
            Folding is genuinely useful and consistently oversold. Understanding
            exactly what it recovers is the difference between a machine that gets
            put away and one that lives permanently unfolded.
          </p>
          <p>
            A folded treadmill does not disappear. A mid-range folding machine
            stands about 5 to 6 feet tall on a floor area of roughly 3 feet by
            3 feet. Compared with the 6 by 3 feet it occupies in use, that halves
            the floor area — real, but a long way from the marketing image of a
            machine tucked behind a door.
          </p>
          <p>
            It also does not fold the clearance away. You still need the 6 feet
            behind it while you are using it, so a folding machine does not let you
            place a treadmill in a space that is too short. What it does is let
            that same floor be a room again between sessions, which is the correct
            reason to buy one.
          </p>
          <p>
            The mechanism matters more than the fold. A hydraulic assist makes the
            difference between a fold that happens daily and one that happens
            twice. On a heavy machine, a manual latch fold is a two-handed lift of
            a substantial weight, and most owners stop doing it within a month. Our
            <a href="/folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">folding mechanisms guide</a>
            compares hydraulic, manual and motorised systems on exactly this point.
          </p>
          <p>
            Also worth knowing: the folded machine has to be parked on a flat
            surface where it will not be knocked, and folded machines on carpet can
            settle unevenly. Plan the parking spot as carefully as the running
            spot.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="upstairs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Upstairs, Basements and Garages</h2>
          <p>
            Three common locations, three different sets of problems.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Upstairs.</strong> Structurally this is usually fine. A
            treadmill plus a user is typically 400 to 600 lb spread over a large
            footprint, which is well inside the design load of a residential floor.
            The genuine problems are getting it up there, floor deflection making
            the machine feel bouncy at pace, and noise. Impact noise travels
            downward through structure far more effectively than airborne noise,
            and this is the most common reason a first-floor treadmill gets moved.
            Our
            <a href="/quiet-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">quiet treadmills guide</a>
            explains why noise beside the deck and noise in the room below are
            different problems with different fixes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Basements.</strong> Excellent for noise and floor rigidity,
            frequently disqualified by ceiling height. Check the height at the
            lowest point over the machine, which is often a duct or a beam rather
            than the ceiling proper. Humidity is the second issue: a damp basement
            is hard on electronics and on the deck, and a dehumidifier is a
            sensible companion purchase.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Garages.</strong> The space is usually there and the
            environment usually is not. Temperature swings cause condensation
            inside the console and motor housing, dust from a working garage gets
            into the motor, and cold makes belt lubricant stiff enough to add real
            drag at start-up. If a garage is the only option, keep the machine
            covered, and let it warm before running it hard in winter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="measuring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Measuring Your Room Properly</h2>
          <p>
            Ten minutes with a tape and a roll of masking tape will settle this
            more reliably than any amount of comparing numbers on product pages.
          </p>
          <p>
            Mark the machine's actual footprint on the floor with tape. Then mark
            the 6-foot zone behind it and the 2-foot strips at the sides. Live with
            the outline for a day. It is a strikingly effective test, because you
            will immediately notice that the door no longer opens, or the radiator
            is inside the safety zone, or you cannot get to the window.
          </p>
          <p>
            Measure the ceiling at the position where you will be standing, not at
            the middle of the room. Sloped ceilings, dropped soffits and light
            fittings all bite here, and the relevant number is the one directly
            above the front third of the deck where you actually run.
          </p>
          <p>
            Check the power situation while you are there. A treadmill should be on
            a grounded outlet, ideally on its own circuit, and extension leads are
            explicitly discouraged by most manufacturers because of the current
            draw at start-up. If the only outlet is across the room, that is a
            constraint on placement, and it is covered further in our
            <a href="/treadmill-electricity-usage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">electricity use guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, check what the machine will sit on. A hard, level floor is
            ideal. Carpet complicates both stability and ventilation and has its
            own set of rules, which we work through in
            <a href="/treadmill-on-carpet/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">putting a treadmill on carpet</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="small-rooms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. If the Room Is Genuinely Small</h2>
          <p>
            Sometimes the numbers do not work, and the useful response is to change
            the machine rather than to shave the clearance.
          </p>
          <p>
            The most effective substitution is a walking pad. At around 50 by 22
            inches and 5 inches tall, it stores under a bed or a sofa, needs no
            ceiling calculation, and — because walking speeds are low — the
            clearance behind matters far less than it does on a running machine.
            For anyone whose honest use case is walking, this is not a compromise
            at all; it is the right product. We compare the two formats directly in
            <a href="/walking-pad-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad versus treadmill</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The second option is a compact folding machine with a shorter deck,
            accepting the stride limitation knowingly rather than discovering it
            later. A 50-inch deck is a walking deck, and if the room can only take
            a walking deck it is better to buy one deliberately than to buy a
            running machine that does not fit.
          </p>
          <p>
            What is not a good option is reducing the rear clearance. If the only
            place a treadmill will go has a wall four feet behind it, the honest
            answer is that the room does not take a running treadmill. Orientation
            sometimes rescues this — turning the machine to run along the longest
            diagonal, or facing it into the room rather than at a wall — and it is
            worth trying with the tape outline before giving up.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much space do you need for a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plan for the machine's footprint plus about 6 feet of clear floor
                behind it and 2 feet on each side. A typical full-size treadmill is
                around 80 by 36 inches, so the honest room requirement is closer to
                12 feet by 7 feet. The clearance behind is a safety margin, not a
                comfort one — it is where you land if you come off the back.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What ceiling height do I need for a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Take your height, add the deck height of the machine, then add at
                least 12 inches of head clearance. Deck heights run from about 5
                inches on a walking pad to 12 inches on a cushioned home machine.
                For a 6-foot user on a typical treadmill that means a ceiling of
                roughly 7 foot 9, which rules out many basements. Incline trainers
                add up to another 15 inches at full grade.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a treadmill fit through a standard doorway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most folding treadmills fit through a standard 32-inch door when
                folded or tipped on end, but many full-size machines are 36 to 40
                inches wide across the rails and will not. Measure the narrowest
                point of the delivery route, including stair turns and bannisters,
                before ordering, and check whether the machine ships assembled or
                in a box.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much clearance do you need behind a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Safety guidance is consistently around 6 feet of clear space behind
                the rear of the belt, and 2 feet either side. This is not about
                comfort — it is the distance a person travels if they lose their
                footing and are thrown backward off the belt. A wall, a piece of
                furniture or a stair opening in that zone changes a stumble into an
                injury.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put a treadmill upstairs or on a second floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually yes, structurally. A treadmill plus user is typically 400 to
                600 lb spread across a large footprint, which is within the design
                load of a normal residential floor. The real problems upstairs are
                getting the machine there, floor deflection making the frame feel
                unstable, and noise transmitted to the room below, which is often
                the deciding factor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much space does a folded treadmill take up?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A folded mid-range treadmill occupies roughly 3 feet by 3 feet of
                floor and stands about 5 to 6 feet tall. It is a substantial object
                rather than something that disappears. Folding saves real floor
                area — often half the in-use footprint — but plan a parking spot for
                it rather than assuming it will tuck into a corner.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How tall does a ceiling need to be for a 6 foot 4 person on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use your height plus the deck height plus 12 inches. For a 6-foot-4 (76-inch) user that is about 7 feet 11 inches on a folding treadmill with a 7-inch deck at flat, and about 8 feet 4 inches on a full-size treadmill whose deck reaches 11.7 inches at maximum incline. A standard 8-foot ceiling is not enough for running at full incline at that height; walk, keep the incline lower or choose a low-deck machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much space does NordicTrack say you need behind a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                NordicTrack and ProForm manuals commonly ask for at least 8 feet behind the treadmill and 2 feet on each side, more than the 2 metres (about 6.5 feet) behind and 0.5 metres each side in a Life Fitness manual summarising the ASTM F2115 standard. For an 80 by 36-inch machine, 8 feet behind means a clear area of about 14 feet 8 inches by 7 feet. Check the figure in your own model's manual.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Planning Treadmill Space</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Three of these cost money and one of them costs rather more than that.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the rear clearance as a suggestion</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Six feet behind the belt is a safety distance, not a comfort one, and it exists because a belt running at 7 mph delivers you backward at 7 mph. A wall, a radiator, a glass door or a stairwell inside that zone changes the consequence of a stumble entirely. If the room cannot give you that space, it cannot take a running treadmill.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the deck adds to your height</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">You run at your height plus 8 to 12 inches of deck, and you need a foot of clearance above that. A 6-foot user needs roughly 7 foot 10 of ceiling, and an incline machine at grade needs considerably more. Measure at the spot above the front of the deck, where the ducts and light fittings usually are.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Measuring the doorway and not the route</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The doorway is rarely the narrowest point. Stair turns, bannisters, landing radiators and the diagonal at the top of a flight are what actually stop deliveries. Measure the whole path, confirm whether the machine arrives boxed or assembled, and check whether the uprights unbolt to reduce its width.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Believing a folded treadmill disappears</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Folded, a mid-range machine is a 6-foot-tall object standing on 3 feet by 3 feet of floor. It halves the in-use footprint, which is worthwhile, but it does not tuck behind a door and it does not remove the need for clearance while you are running. Plan the parked position before you buy, not after.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
